8.1  Processes, threads and async: doing several things at once

TL;DR
  - Processes don't share memory: safe and independent, but talking costs more
    (8.5). Threads are tasks inside one process that share its memory: fast to
    talk, easy to break.
  - Async runs many tasks on few threads: a task gives the thread back while it
    waits for the network or the disk.
  - Parallel work on the CPU is capped by the number of CPUs; waiting on I/O
    isn't. Pick threads or processes for the first, async for the second.

      ps -eLf | wc -l                 # threads on the system
      ps -o nlwp,pid,comm -p PID      # threads of one process
      top -H -p PID                   # one line per thread

On your machine
  Now       14 threads in 3 processes
  Most      supervisor, PID 4100: 12 · systemd, PID 1: 1 · rsync, PID 5000: 1
  Ceiling   126,906 threads, PIDs up to 4.2 million

Pitfalls
  - In CPython, threads don't run Python code in parallel (the GIL): use
    processes for CPU work. Free-threaded builds are changing this.
  - More threads than CPUs doesn't speed up computing: it adds switching.
  - Each thread reserves a stack (8 MiB of address space by default), but only
    the pages it touches use RAM.
  - A crash in one thread kills the whole process; a crash in a child process
    doesn't.

Deep dive

  ▾ What is a thread to the Linux kernel?

    The same as a process: a task with its own registers, stack and scheduling.
    clone() decides what it shares with its creator: memory, open files, signal
    handlers. A thread shares almost everything; a process from fork() shares
    nothing but copies. top shows tasks per process, /proc/PID/task/ lists them.

  ▾ How do Go and async runtimes multiplex?

    They run many lightweight tasks (goroutines, futures, coroutines) on a few
    kernel threads, roughly one per CPU. When a task would block, the runtime
    parks it and runs another on that thread. Switching between tasks happens in
    user space, far cheaper than a kernel context switch, which is how a program
    can hold a hundred thousand of them.

Related
  Commands  ps, top
  Paths     /proc/PID/task/
